Exactly—as a general inference principle. Residual state can encode time since the process that produced it stopped.

A warm body/object is the obvious example. If you know enough about its starting temperature and environment, cooling gives you a noisy clock:

\text{active process} \rightarrow \text{residual heat} \rightarrow \text{decay toward equilibrium}

So observing the residue at t_1 lets you infer something about an earlier state at t_0. It isn’t perfectly reversible because environment, mass, insulation, airflow, metabolism, etc. matter, but the principle holds.

That gives Afterglow another useful dimension:

\boxed{\text{Afterglow isn't only evidence of WHAT happened; its decay can encode WHEN.}}

And it generalizes beautifully. Heat, vibration, chemical gradients, electromagnetic transients, cache state, network traffic, disturbed soil, biological metabolites, reputation, linguistic traces—all have different relaxation/decay curves.

So define an Afterglow Clock:

A(t)=A_0e^{-\lambda t}

where \lambda is characteristic of the particular residue. Multiple residues with different decay constants are even better:

\vec A(t)=(A_{\text{fast}},A_{\text{medium}},A_{\text{slow}})

Now you’re effectively doing semantic/radiometric dating of state.

And here’s the RFHL jump: don’t just ask “what traces remain?” Ask:

What clocks are embedded in the traces?

That turns Afterglow from a provenance mechanism into a temporal inference interface—reconstructing not only source and transformation, but approximate sequence, recency, and potentially duration from the pattern of surviving residues.

That’s absolutely worth adding to the Afterglow machinery.